I think you’re circling a shared-memory feedback loop: part Wikipedia, part telephone game, part memory reconsolidation.

Someone edits the shared account—an article, a story, a photo, a post. Other people encounter that version, then later remember the event through it. Their retellings feed back into the shared account. Over time, the group’s memory can shift, even though nobody sat down and decided to invent a new version.

The Wikipedia comparison catches the network effect: one edit can propagate. The memory piece adds something stranger: people may experience the adopted detail as their own recollection, because memory doesn’t always preserve a clean revision history.

Maybe the missing ingredient is the Mandela effect—a familiar example of people sharing a confident but mistaken memory. Or maybe it’s source confusion: remembering a detail, but losing track of whether it came from the event, a later conversation, or a version you imagined.

A compact name for your pattern might be: the living wiki of memory. Each person is both reader and editor; each retelling becomes a new source for someone else.